        "subject": "LU-21139 ptlrpc: run the ping evictor from a workqueue",
        "message": "LU-21139 ptlrpc: run the ping evictor from a workqueue\n\nThe ping evictor thread is started by the first MDT, OST or MGS\ndevice and stopped by the last one.  pet_refcount and pet_state are\nnot protected, so devices that are set up or cleaned up in parallel\ncan start several evictor threads, or leave a thread that never\nexits.  ping_evictor_stop() does not wait for the thread, so a new\nstart can also reinitialize pet_waitq while the old thread uses it.\n\nReplace the thread with a work item in each obd_device, queued on\nsystem_long_wq by obd_export_timed_evict().  The work holds a\nreference on the obd, so nothing has to be started or stopped.\nRemove ping_evictor_start(), ping_evictor_stop(), their callers and\nthe global list of obds.  The evictor moves to obdclass, next to the\nexport deadline tree that it walks.  Devices are no longer evicted\none at a time.\n\nThe work sets up its own lu_env for each run.  To keep that rare,\nptlrpc_update_export_timer() now queues the work only if an export\nhas timed out.  It used to wake the evictor on every deadline\nupdate while obd_eviction_timer was zero.\n\nTest-Parameters: ignore\nSigned-off-by: Timothy Day <timday@thelustrecollective.com>\nChange-Id: I89a6eb1b793bc8706c237260ecd7dbc16a6a6964\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
  },
